{"causes":["J.K. Rowling's personal experiences with depression and anxiety influenced the writing of Quidditch Through the Ages.","The publication of Quidditch Through the Ages coincided with the rise of the internet and social media, allowing the book to reach a global audience.","The popularity of fantasy and adventure films and television shows in the late 1990s and early 2000s contributed to the book's success."],"effects":["The creation of the Quidditch League and the establishment of the Quidditch World Cup were direct consequences of the book's popularity.","The book's themes of friendship, teamwork, and perseverance have inspired numerous fan communities and charity initiatives.","The book's influence on popular culture is evident in its impact on the sports industry and its references in non-fiction books, films, and television shows."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"j-k-rowling","sourceName":"J.K. Rowling","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"quidditch-through-the-ages","targetName":"Quidditch Through the Ages","context":"Published in 2001, the book was written by J.K. Rowling."},{"sourceSlug":"wizards-chronicle","sourceName":"Wizards' Chronicle","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"quidditch-through-the-ages","targetName":"Quidditch Through the Ages","context":"The book is part of the global wizarding phenomenon, which has captivated audiences worldwide."},{"sourceSlug":"global-wizarding-phphenomenon","sourceName":"Global Wizarding Phenomenon","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"united-states","targetName":"United States","context":"The book's success has led to the creation of Quidditch teams and leagues in the United States."},{"sourceSlug":"j-k-rowling","sourceName":"J.K. Rowling","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"quidditch-league","targetName":"Quidditch League","context":"The book's themes of friendship and teamwork have inspired the creation of the Quidditch League."},{"sourceSlug":"quidditch-world-cup","sourceName":"Quidditch World Cup","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"quidditch-through-the-ages","targetName":"Quidditch Through the Ages","context":"The book's popularity led to the creation of the Quidditch World Cup."},{"sourceSlug":"j-k-rowling","sourceName":"J.K. Rowling","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"popular-culture","targetName":"Popular Culture","context":"The book's themes and characters have appeared in non-fiction books, films, and television shows."}],"places":[{"name":"Scotland","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"United States","role":"Major market"},{"name":"London","role":"Publication location"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Quidditch Through the Ages","type":"Reference article","slug":"quidditch-through-the-ages"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q192258","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quidditch_Through_the_Ages"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Quidditch Through the Ages contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Quidditch Through the Ages is a companion book to the Harry Potter series by J.K. Rowling (b. 1965), published on 12 March 2001 by Bloomsbury (UK) and Scholastic (US), simultaneously with Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them. The book is presented as a real in-universe text — a copy of the book described as being found in the Hogwarts library, 'written by' Kennilworthy Whisp — that describes the history, rules, and teams of Quidditch, the fictional wizarding sport played on broomsticks. Both Quidditch Through the Ages and Fantastic Beasts were published for Comic Relief, with all author royalties donated to the charity.

Quidditch Through the Ages is an example of the 'fictional textbook' genre within the Harry Potter universe — books presented as real in-universe artefacts that expand the world of wizarding Britain by providing detailed fictional histories, taxonomies, and cultural contexts beyond what appears in the main novels. The book describes the origins of Quidditch (from a fictional historical account of broomstick games in medieval Britain), the evolution of the rules and equipment, and the thirteen principal Quidditch teams of Britain and Ireland.

The publication of Quidditch Through the Ages and Fantastic Beasts demonstrated J.K. Rowling's awareness of secondary world creation as a commercial and cultural strategy — the in-universe fictional textbook format allowed her to expand the Harry Potter world without continuing the main narrative, generating new layers of detail that deepened the immersive quality of the wizarding world for fans. The books also demonstrated the enormous commercial power of the Harry Potter brand — both sold in the millions worldwide and made substantial donations to Comic Relief. The Quidditch sport itself became one of the Harry Potter universe's most significant cultural exports, inspiring 'Muggle Quidditch' (now 'Quadball'), a real athletic sport played on university campuses worldwide with a broom between the legs.","subjects":["English Literature","21st Century","J.K. Rowling","Fantasy Literature","Harry Potter","Children's Literature","Sports Culture","Charitable Publishing"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Quidditch Through the Ages is a significant representation of the global fascination with fantasy and adventure, highlighting the importance of imagination and creativity in shaping our understanding of the world.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
